WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.067
I want to begin this hour

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.736
with breaking news out of Japan.

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:05.672
A Japan Airlines plane burst into flames

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.040
after it collided

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.341
with a Japanese Coast Guard

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:10.410
aircraft on the runway. In Tokyo.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.812
This new video shows the moment of impact

00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:13.980
after the passenger

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.549
jet landed at the airport just hours ago.

00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:18.485
And you can see in the video

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:20.186
the Japan Airlines plane

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:21.488
bursting into flames

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:23.390
as it goes down the runway there.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.724
Five crew members on the Coast

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:26.192
Guard plane were killed.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:28.261
The captain is in critical condition.

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.162
The crew were preparing

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:30.630
to fly to western Japan

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.033
to try to help with earthquake relief.

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:34.801
Nearly 400 passengers and crew

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:36.903
were on the Japan Airlines flight.

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:38.505
They were able to escape.

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:40.807
17 people reportedly were hurt.

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:42.175
Let's bring in CNN's Will Ripley.

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:44.077
He joins us live from Tokyo.

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:45.111
So what's the latest here?

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:45.845
What are you learning

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:48.948
Well, just within the last

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:49.649
couple of hours,

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:51.117
the Japanese prime minister

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:52.886
sending his condolences to the families

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:55.155
of those five Coast Guard crew members.

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:56.256
The Coast Guard has a base

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:57.857
right next to the Honda Airport,

00:00:57.857 --> 00:00:59.292
which sits in the heart of Tokyo.

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:00.960
Just 15 or 20 minutes drive

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.562
from where I'm standing right now.

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:04.264
It's one of the best airports

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:05.465
to fly into in Japan

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:07.467
because of its centralized location.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.401
Right on the bay.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:09.903
And you have a beautiful view of the city

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:10.937
when you fly in.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:12.872
But on this very busy day,

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:13.173
a day

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:14.774
that hours after Japan

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.309
was rocked by a massive earthquake,

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:17.811
you had this Coast Guard crew,

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:19.412
six people on the plane total

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.181
trying to get relief supplies

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:22.315
to areas hard hit

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:23.783
that desperately need the help.

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:25.652
And at the same time, you had a packed

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:27.120
Japan Airlines jet

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.122
that was arriving from Sapporo,

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:29.889
which was

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:30.857
right in the prime

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:32.225
of their tourist season up there

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:33.193
on the northern Japanese

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:34.294
island of Hokkaido.

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:37.230
This plane had 367 people on board,

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.432
including eight children

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:40.366
under the age of two.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:42.168
So you had parents with their kids.

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:43.703
You had 12 crew members

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.238
and a fully packed plane.

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:46.406
And just before six P.

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:47.373
M local time,

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:49.209
as this aircraft

00:01:49.209 --> 00:01:50.610
was landing on the runway,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:51.845
the Coast Guard aircraft

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:53.646
was also on the same runway.

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:54.747
How did that happen?

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:55.582
That is the focus.

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:56.616
I'm going to be the focus

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.318
of an extensive investigation

00:01:58.318 --> 00:01:59.285
here in Tokyo,

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.653
a nation that prides itself

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:02.655
on public transportation safety,

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:03.123
a nation

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:04.657
that's never had a single fatality,

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:06.259
and decades of running bullet trains

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:07.694
and hundreds of miles an hour.

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:08.795
And yet somehow,

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.263
for whatever reason, that reason

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:11.364
to be determined.

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:12.632
These two aircraft

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:14.968
had a very, very violent collision

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:16.503
that resulted in a huge fireball.

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:17.670
Both of the plane

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:18.738
planes were in flames

00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:19.873
in a matter of seconds.

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:20.640
And passengers

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.575
who were on the Japan Airlines jet

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:25.011
describe black smoke filling the cabin.

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:26.713
They were terrified parents

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:27.881
trying to keep their kids

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:28.848
as safe as they could,

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:29.716
wondering if they were going

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:30.517
to make it out alive.

00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:31.584
A lot of passengers saying

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:33.253
they just didn't think that they would,

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:35.355
especially when the exit doors

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:37.023
according to the passengers in the rear

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:38.491
and middle parts of the aircraft,

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:39.959
were not working properly.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:41.995
But yet through the front exit doors

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:42.962
and there's video of this

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:44.731
that's emerging on social media

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:46.266
people one by one

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:47.534
streamed out of each side

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:48.701
of the front of the aircraft.

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.670
And in just a matter of seconds,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.939
all 400 or so people

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:54.040
who were on that aircraft

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.742
made it off alive.

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:58.211
17 of them in the hospital with injuries.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:02:59.746
But an extraordinary thing

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:01.181
when you look at the video of that plane

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:01.514
that was

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.683
then very quickly fully engulfed

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:05.418
in flames still smoldering on the runway.

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:06.452
It took firefighters

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:07.587
well over an hour

00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:09.088
to get the fire under control.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:11.124
Haneda Airport, a very busy hub,

00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:11.624
of course,

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:12.592
shut down right now

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:13.593
and likely to be shut down

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:14.627
for quite some time

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:15.695
as they investigate

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:16.930
exactly how this happened,

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.631
as they look into the cause of this.

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:19.199
But still,

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:20.833
the fact that so many people,

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:21.434
hundreds of people

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:22.502
were able to walk away

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:23.870
even as Japan mourns

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.171
the loss of these five Coast Guard

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:25.939
crew members,

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:28.107
it is truly a remarkable moment

00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:29.642
at the beginning of 20, 24,

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:31.744
which has been very rough for Japan,

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:32.912
given the earthquake first

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:33.546
and then

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:35.048
this collision on the runway

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:36.482
right in the heart of the Japanese

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:37.517
capital town.

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:38.751
Yes, certainly

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:40.620
this a really dark period for Japan.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:41.854
But as you say, just remarkable

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:42.855
that hundreds of people

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:44.757
were able to escape those flames.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.292
Will Ripley live for us there in Tokyo.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:47.427
Will, thank you.

00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:47.594
All right.

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:48.728
With us now, CNN aviation

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:49.929
correspondent Pete Montaigne.

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:50.230
And Pete,

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:51.998
when you look at those pictures,

00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:54.734
when you see that plane in flames,

00:03:55.335 --> 00:03:56.402
it's a near miracle

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:57.637
that everyone on board

00:03:57.637 --> 00:03:58.471
was able to get out.

00:03:58.471 --> 00:03:59.105
What do you see

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:00.573
when you look at what I see

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:02.075
and you see the impact there

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:04.143
in the first 2 seconds of that video

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:05.912
is really a wakeup call,

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:07.914
not only for aviation in Japan,

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:09.482
but also around the world

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:11.050
and especially in the United States.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:11.918
We'll get to that in a second.

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:13.953
Japan Airlines Flight five, 16.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:16.189
This was a Airbus A350,

00:04:16.189 --> 00:04:17.991
only two years old,

00:04:17.991 --> 00:04:20.026
came in to land on runway 34

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:21.628
right there at Haneda.

00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:23.096
What is interesting here

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:24.030
is that we know

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.365
from the track of the plane

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:26.833
that this plane was coming into land

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:28.701
and the plane behind it had to go around.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:30.136
We do not know the track

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:31.170
specifically because

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.972
there's no data available from that.

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:35.975
Japan Coast Guard Dash eight airplane,

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:37.277
six people on board,

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:38.544
five of them are dead.

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:40.813
We know that the captain of that flight

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:42.682
is in critical condition.

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:45.385
What is really striking to me is the

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:48.121
communication and the coordination

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:51.090
by the crew that made this evacuation.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.458
So, so successful.

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:53.793
Not just the pilots,

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:55.061
but all of the crew,

00:04:55.061 --> 00:04:55.862
the flight attendants,

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:57.964
especially Japan, prides itself

00:04:57.964 --> 00:04:58.631
on efficiency.

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:00.066
They got these people out

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:02.468
in a very short period of time.

00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:04.604
There you can see the two airplanes

00:05:04.604 --> 00:05:05.538
and this big size

00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:06.639
difference here, a dash.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.574
It is a much smaller airplane,

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:09.008
although it

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:10.443
clearly did a lot of damage

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:12.812
to the front of that Airbus A350.

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:14.180
This is the first time

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.983
an Airbus A350 has ever experienced

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:18.584
a whole loss,

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:19.652
meaning the airplane

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:21.487
is completely destroyed.

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.556
What is clear right now

00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.358
is that there was a huge safety

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:27.060
failure here in a culture

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.395
that is incredibly safety conscious.

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:31.264
The Japanese airlines

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:33.399
had a spate of accidents in the eighties

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:34.133
and nineties.

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:36.002
They really cleaned up their act.

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:37.303
But is also clear

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:38.671
is that this was something where

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:40.840
there was an airplane in the wrong place

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.642
at the wrong time.

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:44.010
And now the question is

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.845
where the confusion took place.

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:48.047
Was it a confusion on the part of air

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:49.415
traffic control

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:50.516
or was it confusion

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:52.251
by one of the crews involved

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:53.953
in that Japan Coast Guard flight

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:55.922
or in that Japan Airlines flight?

00:05:55.922 --> 00:05:57.423
So this is something that investigators

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:58.658
really need to look at.

00:05:58.658 --> 00:05:59.492
And, of course,

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:00.393
they will listen to the air

00:06:00.393 --> 00:06:01.694
traffic control tapes

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:03.029
and the communication there

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:04.697
to see if anything was garbled.

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:06.032
What is really interesting here

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:07.300
in the United States

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.001
is that there has been incident

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:11.070
after incident of airplanes

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.338
in the wrong place

00:06:12.338 --> 00:06:13.740
at the wrong time

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.241
on the surface of an airport.

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:16.576
They're known officially

00:06:16.576 --> 00:06:18.277
as a runway incursion.

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.580
And we have seen seven of these incidents

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:21.681
could so serious

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:23.616
that the National Transportation

00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:25.785
Safety Board has been involved.

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:27.520
JFK, Austin, Boston, Burbank,

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:28.955
the list goes on.

00:06:28.955 --> 00:06:29.822
This incident

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:30.623
really immediately

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:33.192
reminds me of the Austin incident

00:06:33.192 --> 00:06:34.494
where a FedEx flight

00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:35.828
was coming into land

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:37.830
at Austin Bergstrom on February.

00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:39.298
Southwest Airlines

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:40.633
flight was lined up on the runway

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:41.768
ready to take off.

00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:43.970
It was in bad weather conditions.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:44.637
It was dark.

00:06:44.637 --> 00:06:45.805
It was before dawn.

00:06:45.805 --> 00:06:47.740
And the FedEx crew caught the error

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:48.775
and went around.

00:06:48.775 --> 00:06:50.877
In this case, of course, it was dark.

00:06:50.877 --> 00:06:53.079
It was about 5:47 p.m. local time.

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:54.847
Although the weather was pretty good.

00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:55.982
And now the question is

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:57.617
whether or not this Japan Airlines

00:06:57.617 --> 00:06:58.084
flight crew

00:06:58.084 --> 00:07:00.052
was able to see this plane

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:02.321
in front of them and why it was there.

00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:03.823
So a lot of big questions

00:07:03.823 --> 00:07:04.590
here, and investigators

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:05.324
really have their work

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:06.359
cut out for them John.

00:07:06.359 --> 00:07:07.293
Well, let's discuss this more

00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:08.694
and get more analysis on this.

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:10.029
Miles O'Brien joining us now

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:11.230
he's a CNN aviation

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.232
and aerospace analyst. Miles.

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:15.368
Good to see you today, unfortunately.

00:07:15.368 --> 00:07:17.103
Certainly a dark day for Japan.

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:18.538
Well, just your initial read,

00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:20.440
I mean, what do you think happened here?

00:07:21.874 --> 00:07:22.608
Well,

00:07:22.742 --> 00:07:23.176
you know, there are

00:07:23.176 --> 00:07:25.111
many, many layers of safety

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:26.479
built into the system.

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:29.449
And we got down to the absolute last

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:30.016
layer here.

00:07:30.016 --> 00:07:32.118
And this one, which was

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:35.521
the possibility of an orderly evacuation.

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:36.189
And I don't want to

00:07:36.189 --> 00:07:38.257
I think we can't underscore enough

00:07:38.691 --> 00:07:41.461
how successful that evacuation was.

00:07:42.295 --> 00:07:43.563
The crew did a great job

00:07:43.563 --> 00:07:44.197
getting them off.

00:07:44.197 --> 00:07:47.500
And if any group of people collectively

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:49.435
would listen to a flight crew

00:07:49.435 --> 00:07:50.203
and do what

00:07:50.203 --> 00:07:50.670
they're told

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:51.471
and follow the rules,

00:07:51.471 --> 00:07:52.672
it is the people of Japan,

00:07:52.672 --> 00:07:54.407
anybody who's lived there and knows

00:07:54.407 --> 00:07:56.075
the culture there.

00:07:56.075 --> 00:07:57.343
They do, in fact,

00:07:57.343 --> 00:07:58.978
listen to rules and follow rules

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:00.480
so that was really fantastic.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.915
And that was a great demonstration

00:08:03.349 --> 00:08:04.784
of the possibilities there.

00:08:04.784 --> 00:08:06.619
The other thing to think about here

00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:07.954
real is

00:08:07.954 --> 00:08:09.589
there have been decades of work

00:08:09.589 --> 00:08:10.790
on the part of the FAA

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:12.158
and other organizations

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:14.227
to reduce the flammability

00:08:14.594 --> 00:08:16.262
of the materials

00:08:16.262 --> 00:08:18.931
that are inside the cabin of an aircraft

00:08:19.298 --> 00:08:20.766
And this is an example here,

00:08:20.766 --> 00:08:22.301
I think, of the success

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:24.670
there, that it holds off

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:25.605
potential danger

00:08:25.605 --> 00:08:28.107
from the toxic fumes and the flames.

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:29.509
Enough time

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:31.143
to get passengers off

00:08:31.143 --> 00:08:32.278
if they listen to the rules.

00:08:32.278 --> 00:08:35.481
So we can take that away as a half full

00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:36.115
look at it.

00:08:36.115 --> 00:08:37.116
But clearly,

00:08:37.116 --> 00:08:38.251
there is some

00:08:38.317 --> 00:08:40.019
dramatic failure in the system here

00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:41.521
which will have to be

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:43.489
identified and worked on.

00:08:43.489 --> 00:08:44.790
And how do they do that?

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.592
I mean, once they identify

00:08:46.592 --> 00:08:47.159
the causes

00:08:47.159 --> 00:08:47.326
here,

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:47.927
walk us through

00:08:47.927 --> 00:08:50.329
how they officially investigate this.

00:08:50.897 --> 00:08:52.999
Well, you've got three main parties here.

00:08:52.999 --> 00:08:54.433
You've got the flight crew aboard

00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:56.435
the Airbus A350.

00:08:56.435 --> 00:08:57.336
You have

00:08:57.336 --> 00:08:59.505
apparently the surviving pilot,

00:08:59.505 --> 00:09:00.740
the Coast Guard

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:02.208
pilot on the Dash

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:03.676
eight, the smaller aircraft.

00:09:03.676 --> 00:09:06.679
And you have the air traffic controllers

00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:08.948
up there in the control tower.

00:09:08.948 --> 00:09:11.584
And each of them will have a story.

00:09:11.584 --> 00:09:13.486
Now, they'll be interviewed separately.

00:09:13.486 --> 00:09:15.421
And of course, on top of that,

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:16.022
what you have

00:09:16.022 --> 00:09:17.623
are lots of recordings, recordings,

00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:19.625
of the air traffic control communications

00:09:20.126 --> 00:09:22.328
and the cockpit voice

00:09:22.328 --> 00:09:24.463
recorder on the Airbus A350.

00:09:24.463 --> 00:09:25.698
I'm not sure

00:09:25.698 --> 00:09:27.733
if that Japanese Coast Guard craft

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:29.335
was similarly equipped.

00:09:29.335 --> 00:09:30.903
We'll have to see about that.

00:09:30.903 --> 00:09:32.471
But the parties involved,

00:09:32.471 --> 00:09:33.806
the people who made the decisions

00:09:33.806 --> 00:09:35.541
in this case are all present.

00:09:35.541 --> 00:09:38.144
And with us still to sort of sort out

00:09:38.144 --> 00:09:39.378
what happened,

00:09:39.378 --> 00:09:41.314
where the confusion happened

00:09:41.314 --> 00:09:42.915
is, as Pete pointed out,

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:43.749
it was good weather,

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:44.717
but it was nighttime.

00:09:44.717 --> 00:09:46.352
It's the busiest airport

00:09:46.352 --> 00:09:47.620
in the Pacific Rim

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:49.355
in the middle of a terrible disaster.

00:09:49.355 --> 00:09:50.823
A lot of adrenaline

00:09:50.823 --> 00:09:52.725
going, I'm sure, with that Coast Guard

00:09:52.725 --> 00:09:53.893
crew trying to get

00:09:53.893 --> 00:09:55.261
needed relief supplies

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:56.662
up to the stricken region.

00:09:56.662 --> 00:09:57.330
So there are a lot

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:58.864
of subtle human factors here

00:09:58.864 --> 00:10:00.600
that need to be addressed.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:01.067
Mm hmm.

00:10:01.233 --> 00:10:02.368
Miles O'Brien, we

00:10:02.368 --> 00:10:03.135
we appreciate the time

00:10:03.135 --> 00:10:04.070
and the insights today.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:06.005
Thank you, Miles. You're welcome to help.

